WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.797
Picture a doctor called to the ward at 3 a.m.

2
00:00:05.797 --> 00:00:10.408
On the wall hangs a board with forty numbers, in small handwriting.

3
00:00:10.633 --> 00:00:14.716
Some temperatures in Celsius, some in Fahrenheit, nothing circled.

4
00:00:14.716 --> 00:00:17.004
She has one minute to pick a patient.

5
00:00:17.233 --> 00:00:19.766
Every team builds that board by accident.

6
00:00:19.766 --> 00:00:22.237
Each number was added for a good reason.

7
00:00:22.237 --> 00:00:23.781
Nobody planned the whole.

8
00:00:24.500 --> 00:00:30.313
Our version of that board is a dashboard, a web page of graphs drawn by a tool called Grafana.

9
00:00:30.313 --> 00:00:32.602
It shows seven days of request rates.

10
00:00:32.602 --> 00:00:34.333
Where do those numbers live?

11
00:00:39.067 --> 00:00:41.100
Let's watch a page load, and see.

12
00:00:41.733 --> 00:00:42.657
Three players.

13
00:00:42.657 --> 00:00:48.659
Your browser, Grafana, and Prometheus, the tool that collects and stores the app's numbers.

14
00:00:48.867 --> 00:00:57.658
First, the browser loads the dashboard from Grafana: a file listing the panels, the boxes on the page, and each one's query.

15
00:00:57.658 --> 00:00:58.437
No numbers.

16
00:00:58.667 --> 00:01:00.904
Then each panel sends its query.

17
00:01:00.904 --> 00:01:03.352
Grafana passes it on to Prometheus.

18
00:01:03.567 --> 00:01:07.179
Prometheus answers, and the browser draws the result.

19
00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:08.753
So the answer is B.

20
00:01:08.753 --> 00:01:14.590
Grafana stores dashboards, users and settings, but the numbers stay in Prometheus.

21
00:01:14.590 --> 00:01:16.085
Grafana is the board.

22
00:01:16.085 --> 00:01:19.075
Prometheus is the patient chart behind it.

23
00:01:19.300 --> 00:01:26.645
So Grafana cannot show what Prometheus never scraped, or what it already deleted, after seven days in the lab.

24
00:01:26.645 --> 00:01:29.383
And every refresh runs every query again.

25
00:01:30.200 --> 00:01:33.272
Now let's build one panel the doctor could read.

26
00:01:33.272 --> 00:01:37.175
Start from the raw counter of hello-devops, our tiny lab app.

27
00:01:37.175 --> 00:01:39.031
Five hundred and sixty three.

28
00:01:39.233 --> 00:01:41.912
That is the total since the app started.

29
00:01:41.912 --> 00:01:42.916
It only climbs.

30
00:01:43.133 --> 00:01:45.872
Wrap it in rate, and add the paths together.

31
00:01:45.872 --> 00:01:47.925
Now it reads four point four two.

32
00:01:48.133 --> 00:01:49.914
Four point four two what?

33
00:01:49.914 --> 00:01:51.624
The panel does not know.

34
00:01:51.624 --> 00:01:53.548
Nobody reading it at 3 a.m.

35
00:01:53.548 --> 00:01:55.044
should have to guess.

36
00:01:55.267 --> 00:01:57.007
So set the panel's unit.

37
00:01:57.007 --> 00:01:58.820
The data does not change.

38
00:01:58.820 --> 00:02:00.343
Only how it is shown.

39
00:02:00.567 --> 00:02:06.133
Prometheus stores base units: seconds, bytes, ratios between zero and one.

40
00:02:06.133 --> 00:02:08.089
The unit makes them human.

41
00:02:08.300 --> 00:02:12.596
So never multiply by a hundred in your query to get a percentage.

42
00:02:12.596 --> 00:02:14.777
Keep the ratio, and set the unit.

43
00:02:15.400 --> 00:02:17.568
Second panel, the error ratio.

44
00:02:17.568 --> 00:02:19.231
Nothing has failed yet.

45
00:02:19.231 --> 00:02:20.749
So what does it show?

46
00:02:20.749 --> 00:02:22.339
You might expect zero.

47
00:02:22.567 --> 00:02:24.168
Instead it says No data.

48
00:02:24.168 --> 00:02:29.641
The failure series does not exist yet, and a division with an empty side is empty.

49
00:02:29.867 --> 00:02:34.588
Add or vector zero to the error part, and the panel gets a real zero.

50
00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:38.469
Now the red zone, like the one on a car's rev counter.

51
00:02:38.469 --> 00:02:40.915
In Grafana it is called a threshold.

52
00:02:41.133 --> 00:02:47.604
The lab's error ratio is green below one percent, orange from one percent, and red from five.

53
00:02:47.833 --> 00:02:54.319
So a ratio of point zero three four seven shows as three point four seven percent, in orange.

54
00:02:54.319 --> 00:02:56.551
Nobody has to remember anything.

55
00:02:56.767 --> 00:02:59.716
Use color only for what someone should act on.

56
00:02:59.716 --> 00:03:03.948
If every panel is red or green all day, people stop seeing colors.

57
00:03:04.667 --> 00:03:07.556
One more trap, and it hides until you zoom out.

58
00:03:07.556 --> 00:03:09.708
A graph panel asks for many points.

59
00:03:09.708 --> 00:03:15.119
The gap between two points is called the step, and Grafana picks it from the time range.

60
00:03:15.333 --> 00:03:18.959
Here is an hour, with a short spike of errors in the middle.

61
00:03:18.959 --> 00:03:20.531
My spike, for the example.

62
00:03:20.733 --> 00:03:24.464
Zoom out far, and the points land about ten minutes apart.

63
00:03:24.464 --> 00:03:28.001
Each point runs rate with a fixed window of one minute.

64
00:03:28.001 --> 00:03:29.480
Does the spike show up?

65
00:03:29.480 --> 00:03:30.509
Pause and guess.

66
00:03:32.733 --> 00:03:33.860
It falls in a gap.

67
00:03:33.860 --> 00:03:38.677
Each window sees one minute out of every ten, and the graph says all is well.

68
00:03:38.900 --> 00:03:42.299
Grafana's helper, rate interval, grows with the step.

69
00:03:42.299 --> 00:03:46.915
It takes the step plus one scrape, or four scrapes, whichever is larger.

70
00:03:46.915 --> 00:03:49.352
Now every window touches the next one.

71
00:03:49.567 --> 00:03:51.963
And the window that covers the spike sees it.

72
00:03:52.167 --> 00:03:56.172
A fixed range that looks fine zoomed in goes blind zoomed out.

73
00:03:56.172 --> 00:03:58.239
Use rate interval in every rate.

74
00:03:59.067 --> 00:04:00.192
A few guardrails.

75
00:04:00.192 --> 00:04:02.377
Pick the chart from the question.

76
00:04:02.377 --> 00:04:06.084
A line for how it changes, one big number for right now.

77
00:04:06.300 --> 00:04:10.315
For a stat, use Last star, the last value that is not empty.

78
00:04:10.315 --> 00:04:13.460
A mean of seven days hides a ten minute outage.

79
00:04:13.667 --> 00:04:18.412
Lay the page out for the tired reader: is it broken, since when, and where.

80
00:04:18.412 --> 00:04:21.829
Four numbers on top, the same signals over time below.

81
00:04:22.033 --> 00:04:25.145
And every dashboard is a JSON file underneath.

82
00:04:25.145 --> 00:04:29.407
Keep it in git, so nobody's late night edit quietly changes it.

83
00:04:30.133 --> 00:04:32.224
So the moral of the story.

84
00:04:32.224 --> 00:04:33.350
Grafana draws.

85
00:04:33.350 --> 00:04:35.039
Prometheus remembers.

86
00:04:35.267 --> 00:04:38.640
Every panel asks Prometheus again, on every load.

87
00:04:38.640 --> 00:04:41.600
Units and red zones make a number readable.

88
00:04:41.600 --> 00:04:42.977
No data is not zero.

89
00:04:42.977 --> 00:04:45.661
And the window must grow with the step.

90
00:04:46.367 --> 00:04:47.283
A quick check.

91
00:04:47.283 --> 00:04:50.558
The lab's Prometheus keeps samples for seven days.

92
00:04:50.558 --> 00:04:52.391
You open a thirty day graph.

93
00:04:52.391 --> 00:04:55.076
What do the first twenty three days show?

94
00:04:58.200 --> 00:04:58.775
Nothing.

95
00:04:58.775 --> 00:05:04.315
Those samples are gone from Prometheus, and Grafana keeps no copy of its own.
